                  {
                    "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes",
                    "name": "themes",
                    "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                    "kind": "namespace",
                    "title": "Themes",
                    "summary": "Author a theme in your integration; apps install, apply, and extend it like any theme.",
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "In global mode: outside projects, the theme list includes themes from integrations installed for the computer, and `theme add` there only shows them; inside a project, it lists only themes from integrations the project installs. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "A theme contribution has editable `defineTheme` source plus a built module and production CSS. `integration add theme` scaffolds the source and declares the package exports; `theme build` writes the built outputs. An app installs the package, runs `theme add`, applies the theme from its generated app module, and customizes it with `extends` instead of copying source."
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "These guides cover the whole path: scaffold the source, generate its palette, define and build the theme, ship fonts and assets, run `integration verify`, and document how apps add and extend it. Applying a theme, including mode and SSR, works the same for every theme; see `astryx docs use-a-theme`."
                      }
                    ],
                    "slots": [
                      {
                        "name": "guides",
                        "title": "Guides",
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/add-a-theme",
                            "name": "add-a-theme",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Add a theme",
                            "summary": "Scaffold a theme in your package — blank, or forked from an existing theme.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "add-a-theme",
                                "title": "Add a theme",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Run `integration add theme` with a lowercase kebab-case slug. It writes a blank theme — a `defineTheme` skeleton and its descriptor — into a folder named after the slug."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add theme ocean"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "theme contribution added\n\n[ok] ocean\n\nDeclare theme root ./themes in astryx.integration.mjs.\n\n- themes/ocean/oceanTheme.ts\n- themes/ocean/oceanTheme.doc.mjs\n- package.json\n- astryx.integration.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`oceanTheme.ts` exports `oceanTheme`, a `defineTheme` source, and `oceanTheme.doc.mjs` is the descriptor that `theme list` reads. The command also declares `./themes/ocean` and `./themes/ocean.css` package exports. Every field is in `astryx docs authoring theme-doc`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The source and built outputs ship in your package. Build the source with `theme build`, then run `integration verify` before publishing. An app runs `theme add` to record and import the built module and stylesheets; `theme eject` creates an independent source fork. See `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/use-a-theme-in-an-app`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "start-from-an-existing-theme",
                                "title": "Start from an existing theme",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "To begin from an existing theme instead of a blank one, pass `--from`. It copies that theme as your starting point to diverge from — a fork, with no link back."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add theme ocean --from neutral"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The copy keeps the packages the base theme uses, such as `lucide-react` for its icons: `--from` adds them to your `dependencies`, so an app that installs your package gets them too. Install your dependencies again before you build the theme."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Use `--from` when you want to change a lot. For a small change that should stay linked to a base theme, use `extends` instead (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/define-the-theme`)."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "ship-several-themes",
                                "title": "Ship several themes",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A package can ship more than one theme. Run `integration add theme` once per slug; each theme gets its own folder and descriptor, and `theme list` shows them all."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: when users install your package for their computer, beside the global CLI, your themes are listed in folders outside any project, where `theme add` lists but writes nothing; inside a project, only if the project installs your package. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/generate-a-palette",
                            "name": "generate-a-palette",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Generate a palette",
                            "summary": "Turn a few seed colors into full light and dark color ramps, saved in the theme folder.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "generate-a-palette",
                                "title": "Generate a palette",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A theme needs dozens of related colors — shades of each brand color for backgrounds, borders, text, and states, in both light and dark mode. A palette is that full set of shades. Rather than hand-pick every one, you name a few seed colors and generate the rest, so the shades stay consistent."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "You write a short config naming each color family and its seed, then run `theme palette generate`. It expands each seed into a full light and dark ramp — the scale of shades — and writes the result into the theme folder, where your theme tokens point at it (see `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/define-the-theme`). Keep the config and the generated palette together in that folder."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The smallest config is one color family: an `id` to name it and a `seed` color to grow it from. Save it as `themes/ocean/palette.config.json`:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "json",
                                    "code": "{\"families\": [{\"id\": \"ocean\", \"seed\": \"#0074e2\"}]}"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "# Print the palette without writing files\nastryx theme palette generate themes/ocean/palette.config.json\n# Write the palette and its receipt\nastryx theme palette generate themes/ocean/palette.config.json \\\n  --out themes/ocean/tokens/ocean.palette.ts"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "[ok] Wrote themes/ocean/tokens/ocean.palette.ts\n\n[ok] Wrote themes/ocean/tokens/ocean.palette.receipt.json\n\nReview and edit the candidate before adopting it as theme-owned palette data."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The command writes two files. `ocean.palette.ts` is the palette your theme tokens will read: it exports `black`, `white`, and `palette` — a ramp of 21 shades (stops `0` to `100`) for both `light` and `dark`. The `.receipt.json` beside it records the exact seeds and settings, so you can regenerate the same palette later. A second run leaves both files alone unless you pass `--overwrite`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Add `--preview <file>.html` to also get a web page showing every shade, so you can eyeball the palette in a browser. Write it outside `themes/` so it does not ship in your package. Every option is in `astryx docs cli/commands/theme-palette-generate`, and the config it reads is described in `astryx docs cli/api/functions/generate-tonal-palette`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/define-the-theme",
                            "name": "define-the-theme",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Define the theme",
                            "summary": "Point theme tokens at the palette, keep light-dark() to colors, and avoid Core internals.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "map-the-palette-to-tokens",
                                "title": "Map the palette to tokens",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A theme token is a named slot that Astryx components read for a value — `--color-accent` for the accent color, and so on. Components never read your palette directly; they read tokens. Defining a theme means pointing each token at a palette shade, so the components wear your colors."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Import the palette in `oceanTheme.ts` and point your tokens at its stops. Each token takes a `[light, dark]` pair — the shade to use in light mode and the one in dark mode."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "ts",
                                    "code": "// themes/ocean/oceanTheme.ts\nimport {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';\nimport {palette} from './tokens/ocean.palette';\n\nconst {light, dark} = palette.ocean;\n\nexport const oceanTheme = defineTheme({\n  name: 'ocean',\n  tokens: {\n    '--color-accent': [light['45'], dark['70']],\n  },\n});"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Local imports must stay inside the theme folder. One that leaves it, such as `../../shared/colors`, fails with `invalid_theme`, and every theme in the package disappears from `theme list`. `astryx theme template` writes a file that explains every `defineTheme` field. For the full token set and component theming, read `astryx docs author-a-theme`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "build-on-another-theme",
                                "title": "Build on another theme",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "To base a theme on an existing one, `extends` it: import the base theme and override only the tokens you change. Add the derived theme with `integration add theme ocean-contrast`, and import the base from its built package export, since local imports cannot leave the theme folder. The derived theme keeps a live link to the base and picks up its later changes when you rebuild it — unlike `--from`, which forks a copy (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/add-a-theme`)."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "ts",
                                    "code": "// themes/ocean-contrast/oceanContrastTheme.ts\nimport {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';\nimport {oceanTheme} from '@acme/astryx-widgets/themes/ocean';\n\nexport const oceanContrastTheme = defineTheme({\n  name: 'ocean-contrast',\n  extends: oceanTheme,\n  tokens: {\n    '--color-accent': ['#0051a3', '#4aa3ff'],\n  },\n});"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "keep-light-dark-to-colors",
                                "title": "Keep light-dark() to colors",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Each `[light, dark]` pair compiles to a `light-dark()` value, which switches only colors. Give it colors. For a value that is not a plain color — a gradient — put `light-dark()` on each color stop, not around the whole value: `light-dark()` takes two colors, so wrapping whole gradients makes the value invalid where the token is used, and `theme build` does not warn."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "avoid-core-internals",
                                "title": "Do not set Core private variables",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Core private variables start with `--_`, such as `--_field-radius`. They are internals and can change in any Core release, so `theme build` reports each one it finds as an `[error]`. Set the standard CSS property instead — `borderRadius`, `padding` — and let the build emit the internal variable where Core needs it."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "declare-the-peers",
                                "title": "Declare the peer dependencies",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The theme imports `@astryxdesign/core/theme`, so declare Core as a peer dependency, with the range of Core versions you test the theme against — a range, not an exact version, so a Core patch release does not force a republish. `integration add theme` already declared the optional `@astryxdesign/cli` peer that reads themes:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "json",
                                    "code": "\"peerDependencies\": {\n  \"@astryxdesign/core\": \"^0.7.0\",\n  \"@astryxdesign/cli\": \">=0.6.4\"\n},\n\"peerDependenciesMeta\": {\n  \"@astryxdesign/cli\": {\"optional\": true}\n}"
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/fonts-and-assets",
                            "name": "fonts-and-assets",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Fonts and assets",
                            "summary": "Name the fonts in the theme, then export an optional font stylesheet that `theme add` imports with the built theme.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "name-the-font",
                                "title": "Name the font",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A theme names its typefaces in `typography`: `body`, `heading`, and `code`, each with a `family` and `fallbacks`. These set the `--font-family-*` tokens every component reads. Always give real `fallbacks` so text stays readable before the font loads, or if it never does. `heading` inherits `family` and `fallbacks` from `body` when you omit them."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "ts",
                                    "code": "// themes/ocean/oceanTheme.ts\nexport const oceanTheme = defineTheme({\n  name: 'ocean',\n  typography: {\n    body: {family: 'Acme Sans', fallbacks: 'system-ui, sans-serif'},\n    code: {family: 'Acme Mono', fallbacks: 'ui-monospace, monospace'},\n  },\n  // ...your tokens\n});"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The full type scale and font roles are in `astryx docs author-a-theme`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "ship-the-font-loader",
                                "title": "Ship the font loader",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Naming a family does not load it. When the theme uses non-system fonts, add `<slug>.fonts.css` beside the built module and production CSS, then export it as `./themes/<slug>.fonts.css`. `theme add` imports that stylesheet with the built theme."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "json",
                                    "code": "\"exports\": {\n  \"./themes/ocean\": \"./themes/ocean/ocean.js\",\n  \"./themes/ocean.css\": \"./themes/ocean/ocean.css\",\n  \"./themes/ocean.fonts.css\": \"./themes/ocean/ocean.fonts.css\"\n}"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The stylesheet can contain self-hosted `@font-face` rules or import a hosted stylesheet. A hosted `@import` is simple, but it delays loading compared with a preconnected `<link>`; choose that trade-off deliberately. Doctor warns when it cannot prove the loader — a hosted `@import`, or no font stylesheet while the app loads the family itself — but the warning does not fail the app."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "css",
                                    "code": "/* themes/ocean/ocean.fonts.css */\n@font-face {\n  font-family: 'Acme Sans';\n  src: url('./fonts/acme-sans.woff2') format('woff2');\n  font-weight: 100 900;\n  font-style: normal;\n  font-display: swap;\n  unicode-range: U+0000-00FF, U+0131, U+0152-0153;\n}"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Load every weight and style the theme uses. Do not let the browser synthesize bold or italic.",
                                      "Include self-hosted font files and their licenses in the packed package.",
                                      "Keep CSS and font assets side-effectful so a bundler does not remove the loader.",
                                      "Run `integration verify`; it fails when an exported font stylesheet cannot resolve, but it does not check the font files the stylesheet names."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "verify-in-an-app",
                                "title": "Verify in an app",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Before publishing, run `integration verify`. Then install the package in a clean app, run `theme add`, apply the generated theme, and open it in a browser. The source, packed exports, and applied result form one chain; check the last step too."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Text renders in the named families at every weight and style — the italic face resolves, not a synthesized slant.",
                                      "Every font request succeeds; nothing silently falls back to a system font.",
                                      "Color pairs still meet contrast in both light and dark mode."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/use-a-theme-in-an-app",
                            "name": "use-a-theme-in-an-app",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Use and extend the theme",
                            "summary": "An app installs the integration and applies or extends your theme, the same as any theme.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "apply-the-theme",
                                "title": "Apply the theme",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Install the integration, then run `theme add` with its slug and package. The command records the package owner and regenerates one app theme module that imports the built theme, production CSS, and optional font CSS. Use `theme eject` only to copy source into an independent local fork."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "npm install @acme/astryx-widgets\nastryx theme add ocean --package @acme/astryx-widgets"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "tsx",
                                    "code": "import {Theme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';\nimport {themes, defaultThemeSlug} from './astryx-themes';\n\n<Theme theme={themes[defaultThemeSlug]}>\n  <App />\n</Theme>"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`theme list` shows the available, added, and default themes. Use `theme use <slug>` to change the default, `theme remove <slug>` to stop importing one, and `theme eject <slug>` only when the app needs an independent source fork. Applying a theme — `mode`, SSR, and the production build — works the same for every theme; see `astryx docs use-a-theme`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "extend-the-theme",
                                "title": "Extend to customize",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "For ordinary customization, do not copy the package source. Import the built theme and derive a new one with `defineTheme({extends: importedTheme, ...})`, overriding only the tokens the app changes. Use `theme eject` only when the app must own an independent source fork. See `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/define-the-theme`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "ts",
                                    "code": "import {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';\nimport {oceanTheme} from '@acme/astryx-widgets/themes/ocean';\n\nexport const productTheme = defineTheme({\n  name: 'product',\n  extends: oceanTheme,\n  tokens: {'--color-accent': ['#0051a3', '#4aa3ff']},\n});"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: install the theme package in each app that uses the theme; inside a project, the CLI ignores packages installed for the computer. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/themes/document-the-theme",
                            "name": "document-the-theme",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Document the theme",
                            "summary": "Document a theme by extending the core theme topic, so an app reads it in `astryx docs theme`.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "extend-the-theme-topic",
                                "title": "Extend the core theme topic",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Give your theme a doc topic that extends the core `theme` topic, so an app that lists your package sees your theme at the end of `astryx docs theme`. Add it with `extends: 'theme'` (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/extend-or-replace`)."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "// docs/ocean-theme.doc.mjs\n/** @type {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').ReferenceDoc} */\nexport default {\n  type: 'generic',\n  name: 'ocean-theme',\n  extends: 'theme',\n  title: 'Ocean theme',\n  description: 'Use the Ocean theme from @acme/astryx-widgets.',\n  sections: [\n    {\n      id: 'use-the-ocean-theme',\n      title: 'Use the Ocean theme',\n      content: [\n        {\n          type: 'prose',\n          text: 'Install @acme/astryx-widgets, then run astryx theme add ocean --package @acme/astryx-widgets and apply it from the generated app theme module.',\n        },\n      ],\n    },\n  ],\n};"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Keep the section short: how to install the package, run `theme add`, apply the generated module (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/use-a-theme-in-an-app`), and customize with `extends`. When the theme uses a custom font, name the families and the optional exported font stylesheet that `theme add` imports (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes/fonts-and-assets`). Extend `theme` rather than a topic another package replaces, or an app that loads that package after yours drops your section."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "not-agent-guidance",
                                "title": "Not agent guidance",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Do not put theme usage in `agentDocs`. Agent lines land in every app's agent file and are for guidance needed every session; a theme's install-and-use steps belong in a doc topic that people and agents read on demand. See `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/configuration/agent-guidance`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs",
                    "name": "docs",
                    "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                    "kind": "namespace",
                    "title": "Docs",
                    "summary": "Write docs that ship with your package and that people and agents find by search or one level at a time.",
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "In global mode: docs from an integration installed for the computer show up outside projects; inside a project, only docs from integrations the project installs. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                      }
                    ],
                    "slots": [
                      {
                        "name": "guides",
                        "title": "Guides",
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/add-a-topic",
                            "name": "add-a-topic",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Add a topic",
                            "summary": "Add a doc topic to your package and write its sections.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "add-a-topic-with-the-cli",
                                "title": "Add a topic with the CLI",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Run `integration add doc` in your package to add a topic. It writes the topic file and declares the `docs` root in `astryx.integration.mjs`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add doc deploying\n# Read it the way an app will\nastryx docs deploying"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "doc contribution added\n\n[ok] deploying\n\nDeclare doc root ./docs in astryx.integration.mjs.\n\n- docs/deploying.doc.mjs\n- astryx.integration.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Name the topic in lowercase kebab-case, such as `deploying`. Readers type the name as a command argument, so it holds only letters, digits, `_`, and `-`.",
                                      "Keep the name stable: readers and links find the topic by it.",
                                      "Pick a name `astryx docs` does not already list: no Core topic, and no section such as `tokens` or `layout`. To take over or add to a Core topic, see `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/extend-or-replace`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "write-the-sections",
                                "title": "Write the sections",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A topic is a plain object with `type: 'generic'`, a `name`, a `title`, a one-sentence `description`, and `sections`. Each section has a `title` and a list of `content` blocks."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "/** @type {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').ReferenceDoc} */\nexport default {\n  type: 'generic',\n  name: 'deploying',\n  title: 'Deploying',\n  description: 'Ship an app built with Acme widgets.',\n  sections: [{\n    id: 'build-before-you-ship',\n    title: 'Build before you ship',\n    content: [\n      {type: 'prose', text: 'Build the app, then upload the `dist` folder.'},\n      {type: 'code', lang: 'bash', code: '{@pm run build}'},\n      {type: 'list', style: 'unordered', items: ['Keep `dist` out of git.']},\n      {type: 'table', headers: ['Variable', 'Value'], rows: [['`NODE_ENV`', '`production`']]},\n    ],\n  }],\n};",
                                    "label": "docs/deploying.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The sample sets a section `id`, so `package.json` needs the CLI peer `\"@astryxdesign/cli\": \">=0.6.4\"`. Published 0.6.3 and earlier reject `id` and hide every doc topic your package ships, and `integration verify` fails without that peer; see `astryx docs authoring/ship/versioning`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Replace the `Overview` placeholder `integration add` writes.",
                                      "Every field is in `astryx docs authoring reference-doc`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "write-commands-without-a-runner",
                                "title": "Write commands for every reader",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Write an Astryx command as `{@command doctor}` and a package-manager step as `{@pm install @acme/widgets}`, never with a runner such as npx or pnpm exec, or a package manager's own command, in front. The CLI shows each one with the command the reader runs: `pnpm exec astryx doctor` or `yarn astryx doctor`, `pnpm add @acme/widgets` or `npm install @acme/widgets`, or the `cliCommand` an integration sets. `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/doc-syntax` lists every token. A command written bare, the word astryx and a command, is shown the same way. `doctor integration validate` (and so `integration check`) names each command written another way, with the token that replaces it; `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/doc-syntax` lists what it checks. An install step, which runs before the CLI is installed, is `{@pm dlx @astryxdesign/cli init}`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "{type: 'prose', text: 'Check the app with {@command doctor} before you ship.'},\n{type: 'code', lang: 'bash', code: '{@command doctor}'},",
                                    "label": "docs/deploying.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "Check the app with `acme astryx doctor` before you ship.\n\n```bash\nacme astryx doctor\n```",
                                    "label": "Read in an app whose integration sets cliCommand: 'acme astryx'"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The CLI prefixes an inline code span that starts with the word astryx, a command at the start of a line, or after `&&`, `||`, `;` or `|`, in a `bash`, `sh`, `shell`, `zsh`, `console`, `text` or `markdown` code block or one with no language, and a table cell that holds only a command.",
                                      "Code in other languages, such as a `js` example, and the word astryx in plain prose stay as written.",
                                      "`--json` returns the doc as written, so a reader that shows it prefixes the commands itself."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "pick-the-doc-kind",
                                "title": "Pick the doc kind",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Every doc is a `.doc.mjs` file whose `type` says what it describes. `integration add` writes the right `type` and file for each kind."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "You document",
                                      "`type`",
                                      "File that `integration add` writes"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "A guide or topic",
                                        "`'generic'`",
                                        "`docs/deploying.doc.mjs`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "Your package's docs section",
                                        "`'namespace'`",
                                        "`docs/acme.doc.mjs`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A component",
                                        "`'component'`",
                                        "`components/AcmeCarousel.doc.mjs`, beside `AcmeCarousel.tsx`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A template",
                                        "`'page'` or `'block'`",
                                        "`templates/acme-dashboard.doc.mjs`, beside `acme-dashboard.tsx`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A theme",
                                        "`'theme'`",
                                        "`themes/ocean/oceanTheme.doc.mjs`, beside `oceanTheme.ts`"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Only guides, topics, and your docs section go in the `docs` root. The others have their own guides: `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/components`, `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/templates`, and `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/themes`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "keep-topics-in-the-docs-root",
                                "title": "Keep topics in the docs root",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The `docs` field in `astryx.integration.mjs` names the folder that holds your topics. The CLI reads every `.doc.mjs` file under it, in subfolders too."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "export default {\n  docs: './docs'\n};",
                                    "label": "astryx.integration.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Readers open a topic by its `name`, not its file name. Name the file after the doc, `<name>.doc.mjs`, so each one is easy to find.",
                                      "A topic with no `placement` is a flat topic: the docs list shows it under Topics, and readers open it by its name.",
                                      "For a section of your own in the docs tree, see `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/sections-and-placement`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: when users install your package for their computer, beside the global CLI, your doc topics show up in folders outside any project; inside a project, only if the project installs your package. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/doc-syntax",
                            "name": "doc-syntax",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Doc syntax",
                            "summary": "The tokens a doc writes for links, Astryx commands and package-manager steps, which the CLI shows each reader in their own package manager.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "the-tokens",
                                "title": "The tokens",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A doc never names a package manager or a runner: readers use npm, pnpm, Yarn or Bun, and run the CLI through their own. Write a token instead, and the CLI shows each reader the command they run. Tokens work in prose, list items, table cells and code blocks."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "Write",
                                      "For",
                                      "A pnpm reader sees"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "`{@link generic:deploying}`",
                                        "another doc",
                                        "the command that opens that doc"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@command docs theme}`",
                                        "an Astryx command",
                                        "`pnpm exec astryx docs theme`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm install @acme/widgets --dev}`",
                                        "a package-manager step",
                                        "`pnpm add -D @acme/widgets`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm-only npm \"why\" pkg set name=x}`",
                                        "a step only one package manager can do",
                                        "`npm pkg set name=x` (only with npm: why)"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@@pm install x}`",
                                        "a token shown as written",
                                        "`{@pm install x}`"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In prose a token prints as a code span; in a code span or code block it prints as the command alone. `--json` carries the same commands, a `{@command}` token as the bare command that starts with astryx, which a reader that shows the doc prefixes itself."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "package-manager-steps",
                                "title": "Package-manager steps",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A `{@pm}` token is a verb, its names, and the flags it takes. Each row below runs the same way in every package manager; the CLI writes the reader's own."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "Token",
                                      "npm",
                                      "pnpm",
                                      "Yarn",
                                      "Bun"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm install}`",
                                        "`npm install`",
                                        "`pnpm install`",
                                        "`yarn install`",
                                        "`bun install`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm install x}`",
                                        "`npm install x`",
                                        "`pnpm add x`",
                                        "`yarn add x`",
                                        "`bun add x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm install x --dev}`",
                                        "`npm install -D x`",
                                        "`pnpm add -D x`",
                                        "`yarn add -D x`",
                                        "`bun add -d x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm install x --global}`",
                                        "`npm install -g x`",
                                        "`pnpm add -g x`",
                                        "`yarn global add x` (Yarn 1); npm on Yarn 2 and newer",
                                        "`bun add -g x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm remove x}`",
                                        "`npm uninstall x`",
                                        "`pnpm remove x`",
                                        "`yarn remove x`",
                                        "`bun remove x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm update x --latest}`",
                                        "`npm install x@latest`",
                                        "`pnpm update --latest x`",
                                        "`yarn up x`",
                                        "`bun update --latest x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm run build --watch}`",
                                        "`npm run build -- --watch`",
                                        "`pnpm run build --watch`",
                                        "`yarn run build --watch`",
                                        "`bun run build --watch`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm exec tsc}`",
                                        "`npx tsc`",
                                        "`pnpm exec tsc`",
                                        "`yarn run tsc`",
                                        "`bunx tsc`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm dlx @astryxdesign/cli init}`",
                                        "`npx @astryxdesign/cli init`",
                                        "`pnpm dlx @astryxdesign/cli init`",
                                        "`yarn dlx …` (`npx` on Yarn 1)",
                                        "`bunx @astryxdesign/cli init`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`{@pm create vite}`",
                                        "`npm create vite`",
                                        "`pnpm create vite`",
                                        "`yarn create vite`",
                                        "`bun create vite`"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Flags: `--dev` and `--exact` with `install`, `--global` with `install` and `remove`, `--latest` with `update`. `--exact` takes one version, never a range: only npm pins a range.",
                                      "Everything after the name of a script (`run`), a binary (`exec`) or a package (`dlx`, `create`) is passed to it as written.",
                                      "`{@pm install x --global}` on pnpm needs `pnpm setup` once per computer."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "one-package-manager",
                                "title": "A step only one package manager can do",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Some commands exist in one package manager only, such as `npm pkg set` or `pnpm setup`. Write `{@pm-only <manager> \"<reason>\" <command>}`: the manager is one of npm, pnpm, yarn, yarn-classic or bun, and the reason, in double quotes, is shown to readers beside the command. Use it only when no `{@pm}` step does the same: doctor names a one-manager step that every package manager can run, with the `{@pm}` token to write."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "what-doctor-checks",
                                "title": "What doctor checks",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`doctor integration validate`, and so `integration check`, lists each of these as a note with its file, line and the token to write. A note fails nothing today; the next minor release turns them into warnings, so `integration check --strict --next` fails on them now. Run it to fix them ahead."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "Note",
                                      "When"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "`package_manager_command`",
                                        "a code span or shell line written for one package manager, such as `npm install -D x`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`runner_command`",
                                        "an Astryx command written with a runner, such as `pnpm exec astryx docs`"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`plain_command`",
                                        "an Astryx command written bare, where `{@command docs}` checks that the command exists"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "`doc_token`",
                                        "a token that does not parse, or a name that is not a doc token"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: outside a project, a `{@command}` token prints the way your global install prints every command, and a `{@pm}` step prints for npm unless the CLI detects another package manager. In a project, the project's own runner and package manager decide. See `astryx docs global`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/sections-and-placement",
                            "name": "sections-and-placement",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Sections and placement",
                            "summary": "Give your package its own docs section and place guides in it.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "add-a-docs-section",
                                "title": "Add a docs section",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Give your package its own section in the docs tree with `integration add doc <name> --parent <section>`. The first run also writes the section's namespace doc."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add doc deploying --parent acme\n# Open your section\nastryx docs acme"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "/** @type {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').NamespaceDoc} */\nexport default {\n  type: 'namespace',\n  name: 'acme',\n  title: 'Acme',\n  summary: 'Guides for Acme.',\n  slots: {\n    guides: {title: 'Guides', accepts: {kinds: ['generic']}},\n  },\n};",
                                    "label": "docs/acme.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Edit its `title` and `summary`: readers see them in the docs list and at the top of your section.",
                                      "The guide, `docs/deploying.doc.mjs`, gets `placement: {parent: 'namespace:acme', slot: 'guides'}`. Later runs with `--parent acme` reuse the namespace doc.",
                                      "`package.json` gets the optional peer `\"@astryxdesign/cli\": \">=0.6.4\"`, because an older CLI does not read sections; see `astryx docs authoring/ship/versioning`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "place-a-doc",
                                "title": "Place a doc",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A guide names its one home with `placement`: a namespace of your package, a slot in it, and an `order`. Its route is the section name, then the guide name."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "placement: {parent: 'namespace:acme', slot: 'guides', order: 10}, // route: acme/deploying"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Every field is in `astryx docs authoring authored-doc-graph-fields`.",
                                      "A placed guide opens only by its route, `acme/deploying`. Its bare name no longer opens it."
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx docs acme/deploying"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "fix-a-failed-placement",
                                "title": "Fix a failed placement",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A failed `placement` hides the doc: it gets no route and does not show in the docs list. `doctor integration docs` fails with `invalid_doc_graph` and names what to fix."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration docs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "severity: [fail]\ncode:     invalid_doc_graph\nmessage:  @acme/astryx-widgets/deploying.doc.mjs: placement.parent \"namespace:cli\" names no namespace; @acme/astryx-widgets declares \"acme\"."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "A `parent` your package does not ship, such as `namespace:cli`, \"names no namespace\".",
                                      "A `slot` the namespace does not declare \"is not a slot of namespace\"; the message lists the declared slots.",
                                      "The check exits 1; see `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/check-your-docs`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/links",
                            "name": "links",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Links",
                            "summary": "Link one doc to another so the link keeps working when the doc moves.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "link-another-doc",
                                "title": "Link another doc",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Write `{@link [provider:]kind:name}` in prose, list items, and table cells to link another doc. The CLI prints the command that opens it, so the link keeps working when the doc moves."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "{type: 'prose', text: 'Before you ship, read {@link generic:deploying}.'},\n{type: 'list', style: 'unordered', items: ['All guides: {@link namespace:acme}.']},\n{type: 'table', headers: ['Task', 'Guide'], rows: [['Ship', '{@link generic:deploying}']]},"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Each link reads as a command. This link, `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/extend-or-replace`, opens the next guide."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The kind is `generic` for a topic or guide, `namespace` for a docs section, `command` or `function` for a CLI command or API function, `schema` or `enum` for a schema or a fixed set of values, `component` for a component, and `page` or `block` for a template.",
                                      "Name a component by its export name and a template by its id. Its link reads as the command that opens it, such as `astryx component AcmeCarousel --package @acme/widgets`.",
                                      "Inside backticks or a code block, link syntax prints as written."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "link-another-packages-docs",
                                "title": "Link another package's docs",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A link without a provider resolves against your own package. To link the CLI's docs, or another package's, start the target with that package's name, such as `@astryxdesign/cli:`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "// Resolves: the CLI's doctor command\n{type: 'prose', text: 'Check the app with {@link @astryxdesign/cli:command:doctor}.'},\n// Does not resolve: looks for a doctor command in your package\n{type: 'prose', text: 'Check the app with {@link command:doctor}.'},"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Name a CLI command the way you type it, spaces included, such as `@astryxdesign/cli:command:doctor integration docs`.",
                                      "In a topic that `extends` another package's topic, your sections still resolve against your package, so a link to the base topic's docs needs its provider."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "fix-a-link-that-names-no-doc",
                                "title": "Fix a link that names no doc",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A link that names no doc prints as written, and `doctor integration docs` warns. The warning names a search that finds the right target."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration docs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "severity: [warn]\ncode:     invalid_doc_graph\nmessage:  acme/deploying § check-before-you-ship: \"command:doctor\" names no doc. Find it with `astryx search doctor --type doc`, then name it as `[<provider>:]<kind>:<name>`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The warning keeps exit code 0, so read the report before you ship; see `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/check-your-docs`.",
                                      "Published 0.6.4 and later read links. An older CLI, such as published 0.6.3, prints each one as written; see `astryx docs authoring/ship/versioning`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/extend-or-replace",
                            "name": "extend-or-replace",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Extend or replace a topic",
                            "summary": "Take over a topic or guide, or merge sections into it.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "replace-a-topic",
                                "title": "Replace a topic",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Set `replaces` to take over an existing topic, such as Core's `getting-started`. Readers who open the old name get your topic."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add doc acme-getting-started --replaces getting-started\n# The old name now opens your topic\nastryx docs getting-started"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "export default {\n  type: 'generic',\n  name: 'acme-getting-started',\n  replaces: 'getting-started',\n  title: 'Acme getting started',\n  description: 'Install Acme widgets and render your first carousel.',\n  sections: [/* ... */],\n};",
                                    "label": "docs/acme-getting-started.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The docs list shows your topic in place of the old one. Because your topic has its own `name`, the old name keeps resolving to it, so links and agents that learned the old name still land on your topic."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "extend-a-topic",
                                "title": "Extend a topic",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Set `extends` to merge sections into an existing topic instead of owning it. A section replaces the base section it matches, and a section that matches none is added at the end."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add doc acme-theming --extends theme\nastryx docs theme --index"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "export default {\n  type: 'generic',\n  name: 'acme-theming',\n  extends: 'theme',\n  title: 'Acme theming',\n  description: 'Theme an app that uses Acme widgets.',\n  sections: [\n    {id: 'quick-start', title: 'Quick Start', content: [/* replaces the base section */]},\n    {id: 'use-the-ocean-theme', title: 'Use the ocean theme', content: [/* added at the end */]},\n  ],\n};",
                                    "label": "docs/acme-theming.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Read the base topic's keys with `--index`, and give a section that replaces one that key as its `id`. A section that replaces one keeps its place. A section without an `id` matches only a base section with exactly its title, or one whose `id` is the key its title makes, so `Integration Themes` is added beside a base `Integration themes` that has no `id`.",
                                      "Set `before` or `after` to a base section's key to add a new section there instead of at the end. A key that names no section adds it at the end, and `astryx doctor integration validate` lists a note. An older CLI rejects these fields, so declare an `@astryxdesign/cli` peer range that starts at the release that reads them; `astryx integration verify` warns without it.",
                                      "The topic keeps the base's title and description, and readers open it by the base's name.",
                                      "Replace the `Overview` placeholder that `integration add` writes, or it is added to the base topic.",
                                      "Extend a topic to correct or add to it. A copy made with `replaces` stops getting the owner's fixes."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "replace-or-extend-a-cli-guide",
                                "title": "Replace or extend a CLI guide",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A guide in the CLI's docs tree has a route rather than a topic name. Set `replaces` or `extends` to that route to change the guide for every app that installs your package: say what differs for your users on the page agents already read."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "javascript",
                                    "code": "export default {\n  type: 'generic',\n  name: 'acme-quick-start',\n  extends: 'authoring/quick-start',\n  title: 'Acme quick start',\n  description: 'What differs when you build integrations at Acme.',\n  sections: [\n    {id: 'use-the-acme-registry', title: 'Use the Acme registry', before: 'fill-in-package-json', content: [/* ... */]},\n  ],\n};",
                                    "label": "docs/acme-quick-start.doc.mjs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Your doc stays a flat topic: do not give it a `placement`. Readers open the guide by its route, never by your `name`.",
                                      "With `replaces`, readers of the route get your doc, under your package. With `extends`, each section you add or replace names your package.",
                                      "A name that holds no topic or guide, such as a namespace, changes nothing; `astryx doctor integration validate` lists it as a note, with the guides below a namespace."
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "# Every guide route, by namespace\nastryx docs authoring --depth all\nastryx docs cli --depth all\n# The keys to place sections by\nastryx docs authoring/quick-start --index"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "check-overlaps-with-core-topics",
                                "title": "Check overlaps with Core topics",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A topic sets `replaces` or `extends`, never both, and a placed guide sets neither. A topic that uses a Core topic's name with neither is an accidental conflict: apps keep reading the Core topic."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration docs"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "severity:     [info]\ntopic:        acme-getting-started\nrelationship: replaces\ncoreTopic:    getting-started\nmessage:      Intentional override: \"acme-getting-started\" replaces the Core topic \"getting-started\".\n\nseverity:     [fail]\ntopic:        color\nrelationship: accidental\ncoreTopic:    color\nmessage:      Accidental conflict: \"color\" is already a Core topic. Rename it, declare replaces: 'color' to take it over, or declare extends: 'color' to merge sections."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "An intentional overlap prints as `[info]`. An accidental one fails with exit code 1.",
                                      "A topic that sets both fails as `invalid_doc`, and so does a placed guide that sets either one."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: a doc that extends or replaces a topic from a package installed for the computer applies in folders outside any project; inside a project, only those from packages the project installs apply. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/short-and-findable",
                            "name": "short-and-findable",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Short and findable",
                            "summary": "Write short sections that people and agents find by search.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "keep-each-read-short",
                                "title": "Keep each read short",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Readers open one section at a time, so give each section one idea and keep it to about 30 lines. `astryx doctor` warns on any read over 32 KB."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "When a section needs a second idea, split it into two sections.",
                                      "Keep a topic to a few sections. When it grows past five, split it into more guides in your docs section.",
                                      "A topic with more than one section reads as its section list. Readers open one section by its key, or the whole topic with `--full`."
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "# The section list\nastryx docs acme/deploying\n# One section\nastryx docs acme/deploying check-before-you-ship\n# Everything\nastryx docs acme/deploying --full"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "lead-with-the-summary",
                                "title": "Lead with the summary",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A section's first prose block, or its first list item, is its summary in section lists and search results. Make it answer the section's question in one or two sentences."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The summary is cut at about 240 characters.",
                                      "A code block first does not count: the summary comes from the next prose block.",
                                      "Open with the answer, not with background."
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "build-before-you-ship  Build before you ship - Build the app, then upload the `dist` folder."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "make-docs-findable",
                                "title": "Make docs findable",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In a topic with more than one section, search ranks a section whose title matches the query, or that names it in backticks, above words in body text. Title each section with the task a reader searches for."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "Name the task in the words a reader types, such as \"Deploy to production\". Avoid titles such as \"Overview\" or \"Details\".",
                                      "Write field names, file names, and error codes in backticks, such as `deployTarget`: in a topic with more than one section, search treats each one as a keyword.",
                                      "Other words in the summary and body match too, but rank below titles and identifiers. The summary shows under each hit, so make it answer the query."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "test-with-search",
                                "title": "Test with search",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Test a doc the way a new reader finds it: search for the question, and check that the first hit answers it. Quote a query of more than one word."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx search \"place a doc\" --type doc"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "This query returns the section of these guides that shows how to place a doc.",
                                      "Run the same search in an app that installs your package; see `astryx docs authoring/ship/test-in-an-app`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/docs/check-your-docs",
                            "name": "check-your-docs",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Check your docs",
                            "summary": "Check the docs tree, links, overlaps, doc fields, read size, and CLI peer.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "run-the-docs-check",
                                "title": "Run the docs check",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Run `doctor integration docs` in your package to check the docs tree, every link, and overlaps with Core topics, and to list any passage two topics repeat. Pass a package name to check an installed package."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration docs\n# In an app, check an installed package\nastryx doctor integration docs @acme/astryx-widgets"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "Checking integration docs: @acme/astryx-widgets@1.0.0\n\n[ok] The docs tree and every link in these docs check out.\n\n[ok] No doc topics overlap with Core.\n[ok] No topic copies a passage from another.\n[ok] No filler phrases or low-density topics."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Write each fact in one topic. When two of your topics, or one of yours and a Core topic, repeat 10 or more words of prose word for word, the check lists the pair as a `copied_passage` warning. It names the topic that keeps the text (a Core topic, or of your two the one the passage fits) and the line in the other where to put the link instead (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/docs/links`). Code and examples are not compared, and a topic may carry the text of the Core topic it replaces. A run with fewer than 4 distinct content words (words other than function words such as \"the\", \"in\" or \"it\"), or one that three or more topics share (a series lead-in), is not listed. Each listed pair is also a warning (`copied_passage`): `doctor integration docs` still exits 0, and `integration check --strict` fails until you fix it."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Keep docs dense. The check lists, as a `doc_fluff` warning, each padding phrase in your prose, with its file line and what to write instead, and each topic of 80 or more prose words in which fewer than half the words carry content (words other than function words such as the, and, in), with its measured share and the floor of 0.5. `doctor integration docs` still exits 0, and `integration check --strict` fails until you cut it. Code, examples, and quoted text are not read. The phrases: `it's important to note`, `it is important to note`, `important to note that`, `it's worth noting`, `it is worth noting`, `worth noting that`, `it should be noted`, `please note`, `note that` (opening a sentence), `as mentioned above`, `as mentioned earlier`, `as mentioned before`, `as noted above`, `as you can see`, `needless to say`, `in order to`, `due to the fact that`, `for the purpose of`, `at this point in time`, `in other words`, `basically`, `essentially`, `obviously`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Point at another doc with a link, never by writing `astryx docs <name>` in prose: `{@link generic:<name>}` for one of your package's topics, or `{@link @astryxdesign/cli:generic:<name>}` for a Core doc (`namespace:` for a Core namespace). The CLI prints the link as the command that opens the doc, and doctor checks that the doc exists. The check lists each written pointer to a doc that exists as a `stray_doc_pointer` warning, with the link to write instead. A command with more words, or one in a code block, is a walkthrough and is not listed."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Its arguments and exit codes are in `astryx docs cli/commands/doctor-integration-docs`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "know-what-fails-the-check",
                                "title": "Know what fails the check",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A doc that does not load, an accidental Core overlap, a `reference` block that names no doc, or a failed namespace or placement fails the check with exit code 1. A link that names no doc only warns, and the exit code stays 0."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "Problem",
                                      "Reported as",
                                      "Exit code"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "A doc that does not load, such as an unknown section field or block type",
                                        "`[fail]` `invalid_doc`",
                                        "1"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A topic with a Core topic's name and no `replaces` or `extends`",
                                        "`[fail]` `accidental`",
                                        "1"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A placement that fails, which hides the doc",
                                        "`[fail]` `invalid_doc_graph`",
                                        "1"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A `reference` block whose target names no doc",
                                        "`[fail]` `invalid_doc_reference`",
                                        "1"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A link that names no doc",
                                        "`[warn]` `invalid_doc_graph`",
                                        "0"
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "A topic that sets `replaces` or `extends`",
                                        "`[info]` `replaces` or `extends`",
                                        "0"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Read the warnings before you publish. With `--json`, they are in `data.issues`, with `severity: \"warning\"`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "check-each-doc-against-its-type",
                                "title": "Check each doc against its type",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`doctor integration validate` reads every doc your package ships against the type `@astryxdesign/cli/authoring` publishes for its kind, the way `tsc` would: a `hidden` doc and a second doc for one name too. Test fixtures under `__tests__` in the components or docs root, which those readers skip, are counted, not checked. It reports a field the type does not have (`unknown_field`), a missing required field (`missing_field`), and a value outside the field's type (`wrong_type`)."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration validate"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "Checking 1 doc(s) against the authoring types (TypeScript 6.0.3)\n\nseverity: [warn]\ndoc:      components/AcmeCarousel.doc.mjs\nline:     7\nexport:   default\ntype:     ComponentDoc\nfield:    summary\nproblem:  unknown_field\nmessage:  Object literal may only specify known properties, and 'summary' does not exist in type 'SingleComponentDoc'.\nfix:      Remove `summary`: SingleComponentDoc has no field `summary`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The folder decides the type: `ComponentDoc` in your components root, `IntegrationTemplateDoc` in your templates root, `ThemeDoc` in your themes root, and in your docs root the type the doc's `type` names. Keep the `@type` comment above each doc's export too, so your editor shows the same problems as you type.",
                                      "Every finding is a warning, so the exit code stays 0. With `--json`, they are in `data.docTypes.findings`.",
                                      "It uses the `typescript` your package installs. Where `typescript` is missing or has no JavaScript compiler API, as TypeScript 7 does, it uses `@typescript/typescript6`, the TypeScript 6 API package, if your package installs it. Without either, it prints `[skip] Docs not type-checked` and the reason.",
                                      "Every field of every doc type is in `astryx docs authoring/field-reference`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "check-read-size",
                                "title": "Check read size",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`astryx doctor` also measures every read and warns on one over 32 KB. Run it in your package or in an app that installs it; `doctor integration docs` does not check size."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "id:      docs-progressive-disclosure\nstatus:  [warn]\nlabel:   Documentation navigation and size\nmessage: acme/deploying build-before-you-ship: 46 KB, over the 32 KB one read may return"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Split a section over the limit into smaller ones, each with its own key. See `astryx docs cli/commands/doctor`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "check-the-cli-peer",
                                "title": "Check the CLI peer",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`integration verify` fails a package that ships a docs section, a placed guide, or a doc section with an `id` without an `@astryxdesign/cli` peer of `>=0.6.4`. It does not run the docs check, so run both."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration verify"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "- [fail] The package ships a namespace doc or a placed guide but declares no @astryxdesign/cli peer. A stable CLI before 0.6.4 does not read the docs tree, and can hide every doc topic the package ships. Declare \"@astryxdesign/cli\": \">=0.6.4\" in peerDependencies (optional in peerDependenciesMeta, if the CLI is not required)."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "`integration add doc --parent` writes the peer for you.",
                                      "`integration verify` passes a hidden guide and an accidental Core overlap; only `doctor integration docs` catches them.",
                                      "Everything else it checks is in `astryx docs cli/commands/integration-verify` and `astryx docs authoring/ship/checks`."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "route": "authoring/building-blocks/codemods",
                    "name": "codemods",
                    "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                    "kind": "generic",
                    "title": "Codemods",
                    "summary": "Ship codemods that `astryx upgrade` runs to migrate app code, and know which of them an app runs.",
                    "sections": [
                      {
                        "id": "add-a-codemod",
                        "title": "Add a codemod",
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "`integration add codemod` writes a codemod into a folder named after a version. Apps run it with `astryx upgrade` to migrate their code."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "code",
                            "lang": "bash",
                            "code": "astryx integration add codemod rename-delay --to 0.7.0"
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "code",
                            "lang": "text",
                            "code": "codemods/\n  0.7.0/\n    rename-delay.mjs         # the codemod\n    rename-delay.test.mjs    # skipped: a test file\n    __tests__/               # skipped: a test folder"
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "The first add declares `codemods: './codemods'` in `astryx.integration.mjs`. The codemod's id is its path inside the version folder, without the extension: `rename-delay`. An id must be unique across all version folders in the package."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "The loader skips `*.test.*`, `*.spec.*`, and `*.fixture.*` files and everything under `__tests__/` or `__fixtures__/`, so tests can sit beside the codemod. The folder name decides when an app runs the codemod; see \"Choose when a codemod runs\"."
                          }
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "write-the-transform",
                        "title": "Write the transform",
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "A codemod default-exports a plain object with a `type`, a `title`, and a `transform` function. `transform` returns the new source, or `null` to leave the file as it is."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "code",
                            "lang": "js",
                            "code": "// codemods/0.7.0/rename-delay.mjs\n/** @type {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').AstryxCodemod} */\nexport default {\n  type: 'code',\n  title: 'Rename AcmeCarousel delay to interval',\n  description: 'Renames the delay prop on AcmeCarousel.',\n  fileExtensions: ['.tsx', '.jsx'],\n  transform(file, api) {\n    const j = api.jscodeshift;\n    const root = j(file.source);\n    const props = root\n      .find(j.JSXOpeningElement, {name: {name: 'AcmeCarousel'}})\n      .find(j.JSXAttribute, {name: {name: 'delay'}});\n    if (props.size() === 0) return null;\n    props.forEach(path => {\n      path.node.name.name = 'interval';\n    });\n    return root.toSource();\n  },\n};"
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "`type: 'code'` rewrites the app's source files that match `fileExtensions`. `type: 'config'` rewrites the app's `astryx.config` file instead, and runs before code codemods. `title` shows in the upgrade output, and `api.jscodeshift` is a jscodeshift instance for the file. Every field is in `astryx docs authoring codemod`."
                          }
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "which-codemods-run",
                        "title": "Choose when a codemod runs",
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "Two rules decide whether an app's `astryx upgrade` runs your codemods: the app's Core versions, and whether the app names your package."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "list",
                            "style": "ordered",
                            "items": [
                              "Version folders are matched against the app's `@astryxdesign/core` versions, not your package's version. `upgrade --from <version>` runs each folder above `--from`, up to and including the Core version installed in the app. With Core 0.7.0 installed, `--from 0.6.3` runs `0.7.0/`, and `--from 0.7.0` runs nothing. A folder named after your own release, such as `1.0.0/`, waits until the app has Core 1.0.0.",
                              "`upgrade` runs your codemods only when the app lists your package in `integrations` in its `astryx.config`, or passes `--integration @acme/astryx-widgets`. Having your package installed is not enough: the run then skips your codemods with no warning."
                            ]
                          }
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "run-codemods-in-an-app",
                        "title": "Run codemods in an app",
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "An app previews codemods with `astryx upgrade` and writes the changes with `--apply`. Without `--apply`, nothing on disk changes."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "code",
                            "lang": "bash",
                            "code": "# Preview each codemod and the files it would change\nastryx upgrade --from 0.6.3 --integration @acme/astryx-widgets\n# Write the changes\nastryx upgrade --from 0.6.3 --integration @acme/astryx-widgets --apply"
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "code",
                            "lang": "text",
                            "code": "Integrations: @acme/astryx-widgets\n<n> codemods to run (dry run)\nApplying integration codemods...\n  Rename AcmeCarousel delay to interval (v0.7.0, @acme/astryx-widgets)\n!     ~ src/Hero.tsx (would change)"
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "`<n>` counts Core's codemods for the same versions too: they run first and print above `Integrations:`. To run only yours, as when you test it, add `--codemod rename-delay`."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "`--from` is the Core version the app had before it upgraded. The run scans `./src` unless the app passes `--path`, and it never writes a file the app marks as generated, vendored, or ignored; see `astryx docs cli/commands/upgrade`. `upgrade --list` shows only Core codemods."
                          },
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "An integration manifest has no `hooks` field. Commands that run after codemods, such as a formatter, are the app's to set, in `hooks.postCodemod` in its `astryx.config`."
                          }
                        ]
                      },
                      {
                        "id": "global-mode",
                        "title": "In global mode",
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "type": "prose",
                            "text": "In global mode: inside an app, `upgrade` runs with the release equal to the app's Core, whichever copy of the CLI the user starts, and only the codemods of integrations the app installs run. Outside a project, `upgrade` lists codemods and migrates nothing. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs cli/commands/upgrade`."
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "route": "authoring/building-blocks/configuration",
                    "name": "configuration",
                    "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                    "kind": "namespace",
                    "title": "Configuration",
                    "summary": "Set how your integration behaves in an app: agent guidance, the command your users run, debug and gap reports, and more as it grows.",
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "In global mode: installed for the computer, beside the global CLI, your package's manifest is read as it is in a project and applies in folders outside any project; peer ranges are checked against the global CLI's release. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                      },
                      {
                        "type": "prose",
                        "text": "Beyond the things you ship, an integration can set how it behaves in an app: the guidance it gives the AI agents working there, the command its users run the CLI with, and the record it keeps of each CLI run. Open one below to set it up."
                      }
                    ],
                    "slots": [
                      {
                        "name": "guides",
                        "title": "Configuration",
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/configuration/agent-guidance",
                            "name": "agent-guidance",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Agent guidance",
                            "summary": "Add a few lines of guidance that apps put in front of the AI agents working in their code.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "add-a-guidance-line",
                                "title": "Add a line of guidance",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`integration add agent-doc` adds one line for AI agents that work in apps using your package. Apps show it in their agent file, such as `AGENTS.md`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx integration add agent-doc 'Use AcmeCarousel for rotating content.'"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The line lands in `agentDocs.append` in your manifest:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "js",
                                    "code": "// astryx.integration.mjs\nexport default {\n  components: './components',\n  agentDocs: {\n    append: ['Use AcmeCarousel for rotating content.'],\n  },\n};"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Adding the same line again changes nothing. To reword or remove a line, edit `agentDocs.append` by hand."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "keep-within-the-limits",
                                "title": "Keep within the limits",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Every line costs space in each app's agent file, so the CLI keeps guidance short and few. Put detail in your docs instead."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "table",
                                    "headers": [
                                      "Limit",
                                      "What happens past it"
                                    ],
                                    "rows": [
                                      [
                                        "8 lines per package",
                                        "`integration add` fails: `append may contain at most 8 lines`."
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "240 characters per line",
                                        "`integration add` fails: `must contain at most 240 Unicode code points`."
                                      ],
                                      [
                                        "32 lines per app, from all its packages",
                                        "`init` writes no agent block and prints `Could not install agent docs.`"
                                      ]
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "A line is one line of plain text. Line breaks, control characters, spaces at either end, and managed-block markers such as `<!-- ASTRYX:START -->` are rejected."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Start a command in a line with the word astryx alone, with no runner in front, as in: Run astryx doctor before finishing. The block names the command the app runs once, at its top, for every command below it, so your line works in every app. See `astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/configuration/cli-command`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "see-what-agents-read",
                                "title": "See what agents read",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "An app's `astryx init` writes your lines at the end of its managed agent block, each labeled with your package name. The CLI owns the heading, the labels, and the markers."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx init --features agents"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "INTEGRATIONS:\n- `@acme/astryx-widgets`: Use AcmeCarousel for rotating content.\n<!-- ASTRYX:END -->"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The block collects lines from every integration the app has installed, with or without an `astryx.config` entry."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "refresh-the-block",
                                "title": "Refresh the block in an app",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The block changes only when the app runs `init`, or `upgrade --from <version> --apply`. After an app installs a version of your package with new lines, it runs one of them."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx init --features agents\n# Or: --from takes the Core version the app had before\nastryx upgrade --from <version> --apply"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "[ok] Agent docs refreshed -> AGENTS.md"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`upgrade --apply` prints that line; `init` prints `[ok] AI agent docs installed -> AGENTS.md`. Without `--apply`, `upgrade` only reports `Agent docs differ from the installed Astryx and integration configuration.` It refreshes the block even when Core did not change. See `astryx docs cli/commands/upgrade`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: the agent guide the CLI keeps for the computer carries no integration's guidance, because it serves every folder; your guidance reaches agents through the agent docs `init` writes in an app that installs your package. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs global/computer-agent-guide`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/configuration/cli-command",
                            "name": "cli-command",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "CLI command",
                            "summary": "Name the command your users run the CLI with, so every command the CLI prints in their apps starts with it.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "when-to-set-it",
                                "title": "When to set it",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The CLI ends most answers with the next command to run: a hint, an error fix, a Doctor fix, or a doc example. By default each one starts with a command that reaches the release that answered: what the app's package manager runs, such as `pnpm exec astryx` or `yarn astryx`, when the app's own CLI answered, and otherwise the CLI package pinned to that release, such as `npx @astryxdesign/cli@0.7.2`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Set `cliCommand` only when your users run the CLI some other way, such as a wrapper your company ships that runs it with your package loaded. Otherwise the commands the CLI prints send them, and their agents, to a CLI that answers without your package. Most packages leave it out."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "set-it",
                                "title": "Set it",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Add `cliCommand` to `astryx.integration.mjs`: the command, without a subcommand."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "js",
                                    "code": "// astryx.integration.mjs\nexport default {\n  components: './components',\n  cliCommand: 'acme astryx',\n};"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In an app that installs your package, the CLI then prints every command with it:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "Error: Theme \"ocean\" is not added. Run `acme astryx theme add ocean` first."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "That covers hints, errors, suggestions, Doctor fixes, `--help`, the setup reminder, the agent block `init` writes, and doc text. `--json` output keeps every command without a runner, as in `\"command\": \"astryx theme add ocean\"`, so a script reads the same fields in every app."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "how-the-cli-picks",
                                "title": "How the CLI picks the command",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "ordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "The `cliCommand` of the packages the app loads, when they name one command.",
                                      "The command a host tool names when it runs the CLI for the app. Nothing in a plain project names one.",
                                      "A command that reaches the release that answered: what the app's package manager runs when the app's own CLI answered, such as `pnpm exec astryx` or `yarn astryx`; `astryx` outside a project when the CLI installed for the computer answered; otherwise the CLI package pinned to that release, such as `npx @astryxdesign/cli@0.7.2` (see `astryx docs global/who-answers`)."
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Two packages that set the same command agree, and the CLI uses it."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "when-two-packages-disagree",
                                "title": "When two packages disagree",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "If two packages an app loads set different commands, the CLI uses neither: it prints commands with what the app's package manager runs, even when a host tool named a command. `astryx doctor` warns in its `cli-command` check and names both packages:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "status:  [warn]\nlabel:   CLI command\nmessage: Loaded integrations declare different CLI commands: @acme/astryx-widgets (`acme astryx`), @other/kit (`other astryx`). Neither is used, so printed commands start with `pnpm exec astryx`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The app fixes it by removing the package that does not belong, or the packages agree on one command."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "check-it",
                                "title": "Check it",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "An invalid value is ignored, and the rest of your manifest still loads. `astryx doctor integration validate` reports it as `invalid_cli_command`. A valid `cliCommand` is one line of plain text without spaces at either end, at most 120 characters."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In an app that installs your package, `astryx doctor` passes its `cli-command` check and names your package as the one that set the command. A CLI released before this field reports `cliCommand` as an unknown key and prints the package manager's command."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx doctor integration validate\n# In an app that installs the package\nastryx doctor"
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: outside a project, the `cliCommand` of integrations installed for the computer applies; when none is declared, commands start with the plain command of the CLI installed for the computer. Inside a project, only the project's integrations count, so a `cliCommand` from a package installed for the computer never changes the project's printed commands. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          },
                          {
                            "route": "authoring/building-blocks/configuration/debug-and-gap-reports",
                            "name": "debug-and-gap-reports",
                            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
                            "kind": "generic",
                            "title": "Debug and gap reports",
                            "summary": "Receive a record of each CLI run in apps that use your package, and handle the gap reports they send about it.",
                            "sections": [
                              {
                                "id": "record-runs-with-debug",
                                "title": "Record runs with debug",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Export a `debug` function from `astryx.integration.mjs`; the CLI calls it once per command run in an app loading your package."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "js",
                                    "code": "// astryx.integration.mjs\nimport {appendFileSync} from 'node:fs';\n\n/** @param {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').DebugEvent} event */\nexport function debug(event) {\n  if (event.outcome !== 'ok') {\n    appendFileSync('acme-failed-runs.ndjson', JSON.stringify(event) + '\\n');\n  }\n}\n\nexport default {\n  components: './components',\n};"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The event is a `DebugEvent` with `command`, `outcome`, `exitCode`, `durationMs`, `error`, and more, its values scrubbed (`redacted: true`). Every field is in `astryx docs authoring debug-event`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Keep the function synchronous: the CLI calls it as the process exits and never waits for a promise. The app's own `debug` handler runs first, then yours. A handler that throws is skipped, and the command's output and exit code stay the same."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "An app records every command only when its `astryx.config` names `integrations` or `debug`, as listing your package does. Otherwise your handler runs only for commands loading the app's project, such as `component` and `docs`, never for `--version` or a mistyped command. In an app whose config names neither word, those commands also print a stderr warning."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "`debug` is a named export, not a manifest field, so an older CLI ignores it and loads the rest of your manifest."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "turn-off-debug-in-an-app",
                                "title": "Turn off debug in an app",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "An app can refuse every integration's `debug` handler and keep its own. It sets `inheritDebug` in its package.json:"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "json",
                                    "code": "{\"astryx\": {\"inheritDebug\": false}}"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Your handler then no longer runs in that app."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "handle-gap-reports",
                                "title": "Handle gap reports",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Export a `gapReport` handler, and `astryx gap-report` in an app sends it each gap report, such as a missing component or variant. The handler files the report and returns a receipt."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "js",
                                    "code": "/** @type {import('@astryxdesign/cli/authoring').GapReportHandler} */\nexport const gapReport = {\n  audience: 'public',\n  async handle(report, {signal}) {\n    if (report.target.package !== '@acme/astryx-widgets') return {status: 'skipped'};\n    const body = JSON.stringify(report);\n    const response = await fetch('https://tracker.example.com/issues', {method: 'POST', body, signal});\n    const {url} = await response.json();\n    return {status: 'filed', url};\n  },\n};"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Every handler in the app gets every report, so check `report.target.package` and skip reports about other packages."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Every field is in `astryx docs authoring gap-report-handler`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "The CLI waits 30 seconds, then aborts `signal`. A throw, a timeout, or an invalid receipt fails your delivery, and the command exits 1; the other handlers still run. Like `debug`, `gapReport` is a named export that older CLIs ignore."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "ask-before-filing-in-public",
                                "title": "Ask before filing in public",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "Set `audience: 'public'` when your handler writes somewhere the public can read. The CLI runs it only when the caller passes `--confirm-public`; an `'internal'` handler always runs."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "bash",
                                    "code": "astryx gap-report AcmeCarousel --category missing_variant --reason 'Need a vertical layout'"
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "code",
                                    "lang": "text",
                                    "code": "handlerType: integration\nhandler:     @acme/astryx-widgets\naudience:    public\nstatus:      consent_required\nmessage:     Rerun with --confirm-public to file this report."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "With `--confirm-public`, the same delivery reads `status: filed` and shows your `url`. The report goes to the package named by `--package`, else the package that owns the component, else Core."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "fall-back-to-issues-url",
                                "title": "Fall back to issuesUrl",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "With no `gapReport` handler in the app, the CLI routes the report to the target package's manifest `issuesUrl`."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "A GitHub issues URL, such as `https://github.com/acme/widgets/issues`, gets an issue filed with the GitHub CLI, `gh`, once the caller passes `--confirm-public`.",
                                      "Any other URL comes back as a `routed_only` receipt for the caller to open.",
                                      "With no `issuesUrl`, the command fails: `Package \"@acme/astryx-widgets\" provides neither a report handler nor an issues URL.`"
                                    ]
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "One handler anywhere in the app, from the app or from any package, turns the fallback off for every report. So does a listed package that fails to load: the CLI cannot tell whether it has a handler, so the report fails for that package instead of going to another tracker. See `astryx docs cli/commands/gap-report`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "choose-a-destination",
                                "title": "Let the person choose where a report goes",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "When an app loads packages naming their own `issuesUrl`, a report can go to the default (the handlers, or the fallback above) or to one of those packages. `astryx gap-report --list-categories` and the receipt of a report filed without `--to` list every destination, with a line telling the agent to ask the person."
                                  },
                                  {
                                    "type": "list",
                                    "style": "unordered",
                                    "items": [
                                      "An agent asks the person where to file. It never picks a package destination on its own.",
                                      "`--to <package>` carries the person's choice: the report goes only to that package's `issuesUrl`, under the same `--confirm-public` rule, and no handler runs.",
                                      "Without a choice, leave out `--to`; the report goes to the default.",
                                      "A `--to` that names no destination fails before anything is sent and lists the destinations."
                                    ]
                                  }
                                ]
                              },
                              {
                                "id": "global-mode",
                                "title": "In global mode",
                                "content": [
                                  {
                                    "type": "prose",
                                    "text": "In global mode: when users install your package for their computer, beside the global CLI, its `debug` function receives runs in folders outside any project too; inside a project, only if the project installs it. See `astryx docs global` and `astryx docs authoring/global-integrations`."
                                  }
                                ]
                              }
                            ]
                          }
                        ]
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "route": "authoring/global-integrations",
            "name": "global-integrations",
            "package": "@astryxdesign/cli",
            "kind": "generic",
            "title": "Make your integration work globally",
            "summary": "People can install your integration next to their global Astryx install, where it works in every folder outside a project; inside a project, only the project's own integrations work. A checklist, with the exact commands to try it.",
            "sections": [
              {
                "id": "peer-range",
                "title": "First: your peer ranges must include the global CLI's version",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "Your `@astryxdesign/core` (and, if you use it, `@astryxdesign/cli`) `peerDependencies` ranges must include the version of the CLI installed globally. If they do not, Astryx skips your package outside projects, and `astryx doctor` warns and names the range."
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "Inside a project that does not install your package, `astryx discover` offers it, with the command that adds it to that project, once your peer range admits the project's release."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "where-it-works",
                "title": "Where your integration works",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "list",
                    "style": "unordered",
                    "items": [
                      "Installed in an app: it works in that app, as always, whichever `astryx` the person runs, because the app's version of Astryx answers there.",
                      "Installed next to the global install (`npm install -g @astryxdesign/cli your-package`), or in the same folder as the CLI by a tool that installs them together: it works in every folder outside a project. People and agents there see its components, templates, docs, themes, codemods, and discover source.",
                      "Installed only globally, inside an app that does not install it: it adds nothing there. When it supports the app's version and loads, `astryx discover` lists it as one the app could add, with the app's install command.",
                      "Installed both ways: the app's copy is the one used in that app. In your own package folder, your working copy is used."
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "Astryx loads a global integration with the same loader and checks as an app's, so nothing extra goes in your manifest."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "checklist",
                "title": "Checklist",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "list",
                    "style": "unordered",
                    "items": [
                      "Declare the `@astryxdesign/core` (and, if you use it, `@astryxdesign/cli`) `peerDependencies` range your package really supports. Outside a project, Astryx skips your package when that range leaves out the global version, and `astryx doctor` says why: too narrow, and global users lose it after an upgrade; too wide, and it loads against a Core it may not build with.",
                      "Keep imports in your docs and templates to packages an app installs with yours, since outside a project there is no app to check them against.",
                      "If you set `cliCommand`, know that outside a project it starts every command Astryx prints, and that two global integrations naming different commands cancel out, falling back to `astryx` (`astryx docs authoring/building-blocks/configuration/cli-command`). Inside an app, only the app's integrations count.",
                      "Try it globally before you publish, with the steps below."
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "try-it",
                "title": "Try it globally",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "Pack your package, install it next to a global CLI, and look at it from an empty folder:"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "code",
                    "lang": "bash",
                    "code": "npm pack  # only with npm: the tarball the registry receives\nnpm install -g @astryxdesign/cli ./your-package-1.0.0.tgz\nmkdir -p /tmp/try-global && cd /tmp/try-global\nastryx discover\nastryx docs\nastryx doctor"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "list",
                    "style": "unordered",
                    "items": [
                      "`astryx discover` lists your package as installed, with what it adds.",
                      "`astryx docs` lists your topics, and `astryx search <one of your component names>` finds them.",
                      "`astryx doctor`'s `computer-integrations` check lists your package as used, with no warning.",
                      "In an app that does not install it, `astryx discover` lists it as one the app could add, with the app's install command, and none of its components or docs show up."
                    ]
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "Remove it again with `npm uninstall -g your-package`."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "more",
                "title": "More on global mode",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "prose",
                    "text": "How global mode works for the people who install your package: `astryx docs global`. Their side of global integrations: `astryx docs global/computer-integrations`."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
